<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maharaja Sayajirao University of Baroda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as MSU Baroda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> Sayajirao University of Baroda is a public university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vadodara">Vadodara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, founded in 1949 and named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii">Sayajirao Gaekwad III</a>. It grew out of Baroda College, established in 1881, and was created on the initiative of the princely family, fulfilling Sayajirao's plan for a university. It is known for its faculty of fine arts and its English-medium teaching.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maharaja-sayajirao-university-of-baroda.jpg" alt="Maharaja Sayajirao University of Baroda" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dhvani Jadav · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schematic_Map_of_The_Maharaja_Sayajirao_University_(MSU_Vadodara).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Baroda College was founded by Baroda State in 1881, one of the first public acts of Maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii">Sayajirao Gaekwad III</a>, and taught arts and science. Its main building, now the Faculty of Arts, was designed by Robert Fellowes Chisholm in the Indo-Saracenic style, with Indian and Byzantine arches and domes in brick and polychrome stone; the dome of its convocation hall was modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gol-gumbaz">Gol Gumbaz</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>. In 1949 Pratap Singh Gaekwad, the last Maharaja of Baroda, founded the university on his grandfather's wishes and settled the Sir Sayajirao Diamond Jubilee and Memorial Trust for the education of the people of the former state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Several faculties are older than the university. The Academy of Indian Music, founded by Ustad Moula Bux under Sayajirao's patronage on 26 February 1886, became the Music College and then the Faculty of Performing Arts; after Vishnu Narayan Bhatkhande's curriculum was introduced there, Madhusudan Joshi received a diploma in music in 1932. Bharatanatyam came to Baroda with Maharani Chimnabai I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Tanjore</a>, who married Sayajirao in 1880, and in 1950 the university began the first dance programme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Education dates from 1935, when it grew out of a teacher training college. The Faculty of Science became independent in March 1951 under its first dean, C. S. Patel, and the Department of Biochemistry was set up in 1955 under C. V. Ramakrishnan. The Baroda Medical College, attached to the Sir Sayajirao General Hospital, began in 1949, and the Faculty of Management Studies in 1984.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The university has 89 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> on six campuses, two rural and four urban, covering 275 acres. The Hansa Mehta Library was founded in 1950 and keeps a number of titles now out of print. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-institute">Oriental Institute</a>, founded on 1 September 1927 from a plan Sayajirao made in 1893, keeps his collection of more than 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and published a critical edition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> in seven volumes between 1951 and 1975. Its teachers have included the sociologist M. N. Srinivas and the political scientist Rajni Kothari, and its alumni the film-maker Dadasaheb Phalke and Vinoba Bhave.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maharaja Sayajirao University of Baroda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A critical edition was compiled in the 1960s and 1970s by the Oriental Institute at <b>Maharaja Sayajirao University of Baroda</b> from dozens of manuscripts, and Robert P. Goldman completed its English translation in 2016.</blockquote><a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lakshmi Vilas Palace and the <b>Maharaja Sayajirao University</b> are its best-known legacies.</blockquote><a href="/state/baroda-state">Baroda State</a></li></ul></section>
